  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 1.2. Increased institutional capacity of workers&#x2019; organizations</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">1. In May 2020, central and state level trade unions prepared joint recommendations for better labour and employment policies, including for the new Labour Codes and Rules, were submitted to the government.  However, the government refused to dialogue with the unions and issued a package of consolidated and codified national labour law in September 2020 that did not consider the trade union recommendations.   

2. Despite the setbacks, trade Unions continue to demonstrate their relevance in the national and State level response and reconstruction programmes regarding impact of COVID-19 crisis by participating in mechanisms and processes at various levels and by continuing to submit joint position papers and other activities towards achieve a better normal.  The unions have remained united and act as one voice in these mechanisms despite organizational and ideological differences.  Their demands have been jointly agreed and pursued.
 1. ILO provided technical support to TUs in strengthening understanding on labour codes and rules. Trade Unions in most of the cases submitted their joint recommendations to the government.  2. In view of the COVID-19 and its impact on workers and the Trade unions, TUs expressed the need to establish Workers Information and Support Centres (WISC) in some of the selected States. As starting point 4 such centres (Tamil Nadu, Maharashtra, Bihar and Delhi) are being set up  to focus on providing a strategic approach through service delivery, advocacy and organizing to strengthen the workers and the unions to effectively address the challenges and opportunities brought about by COVID-19. The centres are run by the Joint Platform of TUs at the state level where one Central Trade Union takes responsibility to coordinate and implement.  3. A background paper has been prepared by the ILO, to support TUs to structure their COVID-19 interventions based on evidence. ILO is also supporting TUs in taking advocacy efforts for this including study undertaken towards &#x2018; Realizing potentials of Industrial Clusters of Labour Surplus States: For Inclusive Development and Decent Work&#x2019; (ongoing). 4. As a response to the impact of the pandemic and the need to strengthen bi partite relations at the work place a set of Guide lines were developed by the ILO. Subsequently efforts were made to build capacity of both TUs and EBMOs to take this initiative forward. This activity involved a TOT conducted in collaboration with ITC-Turin (13 TU representatives trained). 5. In order to enhance TUs capacities on SDGs and UN processes, series of consultations, discussions and trainings have been initiated by the ILO.</narrative>
   </description>
